# agentlib-service

A Terraform module that runs one agentlib service on ECS Fargate.

`agentlib deploy SERVICE` copies it here and writes a root module for the service
under `deploy/SERVICE/` that calls it. Read that folder's README first.

This folder is agentlib's: every `agentlib deploy` rewrites it, so an upgrade of the
library brings its fixes. Change what it does through its variables, or copy it
under another name and point your root module at the copy.

Its tests need no AWS account: `terraform init -backend=false && terraform test`.

What it makes:

- a task definition with the service, an OPA sidecar when `opa_image` is set, and
  an OpenTelemetry collector sidecar when `collector` is true;
- an ECS service with a deployment circuit breaker that rolls back a bad release;
- a task role with exactly the permissions in `task_policy_json` (which
  `agentlib deploy` derives from the adapters the service's settings select),
  plus what the collector needs to send traces to X-Ray and metrics to CloudWatch;
- an execution role that can pull images and write this service's logs only;
- a log group, `/agentlib/NAME`.

Every container has a read-only root file system; `/tmp` is the writable place.
Fargate creates that volume owned by root, so a short `volume-init` container runs
first, as root, makes it writable the way `/tmp` always is, and exits; the service,
which runs as an unprivileged user, starts only once it has succeeded.
OPA listens on the task's loopback address only. The tasks get no public IP.

It does not make the cluster, the network, the load balancer, the image
repositories, the secrets, or the AWS resources the adapters use: those belong
to the platform the service is deployed into.
